    November 21, 2025 

\vspace{10pt}

    The Birth of a Nation: Influences of American Democracy 

\vspace{10pt}

    On July 4, 1776, the Founding Fathers put a document to the beginning of a brand-new 

\vspace{10pt}

    nation: The Declaration of Independence. This document encapsulated the brilliant hopes and 

\vspace{10pt}

    dreams of a brand-new America. However, many questions came along with establishing a new 

\vspace{10pt}

    nation: How would all Americans be represented in government? How could America be strong 

\vspace{10pt}

    and unified without violating the rights of individuals? To answer these questions, the Founding 

\vspace{10pt}

    Fathers looked to multiple sources of inspiration. As a result, the development of U.S. 

\vspace{10pt}

    democracy was influenced by English documents, the Roman Republic, and the Iroquois 

\vspace{10pt}

\begin{figure}[h]

\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{/convert/6g1V8hj/localstore/rican-government-essay-google-docsassets/3.jpg}

\centering

\end{figure}

    One major influence of U.S. democracy was English documents such as the Magna Carta 

\vspace{10pt}

    and English Bill of Rights. Both of these documents set out to protect the rights of individuals in 

\vspace{10pt}

    a time where the monarchy had absolute power. In 1215, King John was forced by noblemen to 

\vspace{10pt}

    sign the Magna Carta, a document that limited the monarch’s power. From the Magna Carta, 

\vspace{10pt}

    nobles also secured many undeniable rights, such as the right to a due process of law. This meant 

\vspace{10pt}

    that they would have access to a fair trial before being given a punishment. Centuries later in 

\vspace{10pt}

    1689, the English Parliament passed the English Bill of Rights. Signed by the monarchs William 

\vspace{10pt}

    and Mary, the English Bill of Rights went a step further in protecting the rights of citizens from 

\vspace{10pt}

\par

\vspace{10pt}

    the government, including the right to petition, the right to bear arms, and freedom from cruel 

\vspace{10pt}

    and unusual punishment. These documents played a major role in establishing the idea that the 

\vspace{10pt}

    government should not be all-powerful and cannot violate certain individual rights of citizens. 

\vspace{10pt}

    The influences of these two documents can still be seen in the U.S. Bill of Rights. The first ten 

\vspace{10pt}

    amendments of the Constitution protect the inviolable rights of American citizens, such as 

\vspace{10pt}

    freedom of speech, the right to bear arms, and the right of trial by jury. It is universally agreed 

\vspace{10pt}

    that English documents like the Magna Carta and the English Bill of Rights were major 

\vspace{10pt}

    proponents in inspiring the ideas of limited government and individual rights in the U.S. 

\vspace{10pt}

\begin{figure}[h]

\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{/convert/6g1V8hj/localstore/rican-government-essay-google-docsassets/4.jpg}

\centering

\end{figure}

    U.S. democracy was also deeply inspired by Ancient Rome. The Roman Republic was a 

\vspace{10pt}

    huge nation stretching from Britain to Egypt, with people of different cultures, opinions, and 

\vspace{10pt}

    political interests. To make fair decisions representative of all of its citizens, Ancient Rome 

\vspace{10pt}

    adopted a representative democracy. The Roman Senate consisted of 300 senators who each 

\vspace{10pt}

    represented one province within the empire. Although citizens didn’t directly vote on decisions 

\vspace{10pt}

    themselves, they were able to vote on a representative who voted on their behalf. This type of 

\vspace{10pt}

    government works when a nation is too large for all the citizens to directly vote on issues, like 

\vspace{10pt}

    the United States. The Roman idea of representative democracy is reflected in the United States 

\vspace{10pt}

    when citizens of each state elect their own representatives to represent their opinions in 

\vspace{10pt}

    Congress. This way, the voices of all the people can still be heard in government, while still 

\vspace{10pt}

    ensuring efficiency. Representative democracy is still a fundamental part of the government 

\vspace{10pt}

    system in America today. 

\vspace{10pt}

    Furthermore, U.S. democracy borrowed many ideas from the Iroquois Confederacy. This 

\vspace{10pt}

    was an alliance between the Native American tribes Mohawk, Oneida, Onondaga, Cayuga, and 

\vspace{10pt}

\par

\vspace{10pt}

    Seneca in present-day State of New York. While each tribe would settle matters pertaining to 

\vspace{10pt}

    itself, the Iroquois Confederacy met together at the Grand Council to jointly make decisions that 

\vspace{10pt}

    would affect all the tribes, such as foreign trade policies, foreign diplomatic policies, and 

\vspace{10pt}

    inter-tribe conflicts. This is an example of federalism, a government system in which there is a 

\vspace{10pt}

    central federal government ruling over smaller state governments. In the United States, the 

\vspace{10pt}

    federal government has the ability to declare war, regulate currency, and conduct foreign affairs. 

\vspace{10pt}

    On the other hand, state governments in the United States have the power to conduct elections, 

\vspace{10pt}

    oversee education, and issue licenses. The struggle between federal and state governments was a 

\vspace{10pt}

    hotly debated topic at the founding of our nation. Some Americans felt the need for a strong, 

\vspace{10pt}

    united government, while others were reluctant in having the central government become too 

\vspace{10pt}

    powerful. The balance of power between federal and state governments is important because it 

\vspace{10pt}

    achieves a middle ground in between these two beliefs. In summary, the Iroquois Confederacy 

\vspace{10pt}

    inspired the concept of federalism within the United States. 

\vspace{10pt}

    English, Roman, and Iroquois influences are all very much evident in the modern United 

\vspace{10pt}

    States. The Magna Carta and the English Bill of Rights established the ideas of limited 

\vspace{10pt}

    government and individual rights. Ancient Rome created a model for representation within 

\vspace{10pt}

    decision making. The Iroquois Confederacy instilled the idea of federalism. As it approaches its 

\vspace{10pt}

    250th birthday, the United States has gone on to inspire many other governments. To this day, the 

\vspace{10pt}

    hopes of the Founding Fathers still live on, guiding us step by step as we move forward as a 

\vspace{10pt}

